public class Rectangle {
    protected int width, height;
    public void setWidth(int w)  { width = w; }
    public void setHeight(int h) { height = h; }
    public int area() { return width * height; }
}
public class Square extends Rectangle {
    @Override public void setWidth(int w)  { width = height = w; }  // breaks contract
    @Override public void setHeight(int h) { width = height = h; }  // breaks contract
}
// Code written for Rectangle assumes setWidth and setHeight are independent:
Rectangle r = new Square();
r.setWidth(5);
r.setHeight(10);
assert r.area() == 50;  // FAILS — returns 100. Square is not substitutable for Rectangle.
```

```java
// Option A: shared interface, no inheritance between Square and Rectangle
public interface Shape { int area(); }
public class Rectangle implements Shape { /* independent w, h */ }
public class Square implements Shape { /* single side field */ }
// No code can treat Square as a Rectangle and hit the contract violation.

// Option B: Square HAS-A set of sides via composition, not IS-A Rectangle
// The point: if the subtype can't honor the supertype's contract, the
// inheritance relationship is wrong. Fix the hierarchy, not the subclass.
```

