```java
class User {
    private final String email;
    User(String email) { this.email = email; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof User u)) return false;
        return email.equals(u.email);
    }
    // NOTE: no hashCode() override — inherits Object.hashCode() (identity-based)
}

Map<User, String> cache = new HashMap<>();
cache.put(new User("a@b.com"), "Alice");
String name = cache.get(new User("a@b.com")); // returns null — bug!
```

Why: the two `User` instances are **equal** (same email) but their `Object.hashCode()` returns different identity-based values. `put()` stored under hash 12345; `get()` searches hash 98765 — wrong bucket, no entry found, returns null. Every `HashMap` operation on this class is broken.

```java
import java.util.Objects;

class User {
    private final String email;
    private final String name;

    User(String email, String name) { this.email = email; this.name = name; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof User u)) return false;
        return Objects.equals(email, u.email) && Objects.equals(name, u.name);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(email, name); // must include the same fields as equals
    }
}
```

**Key constraints:**
- Include the **same fields** in both methods — mismatch breaks the contract.
- Use **immutable fields** (`final`) — if a field used in the hash mutates while the object is a map key, the object becomes unreachable.
- Or use a **Java 14+ `record`**, which generates both methods automatically: `record User(String email, String name) {}`.
